WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:08.930
. There were many compelling moments sprinkled
throughout the hours and hours of presentation.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:14.200
During Donald Trump's second impeachment trial,
one of the most interesting came during the

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.080
portion of the trial when senators can send
up questions to be answered by either or both

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.880
the House impeachment managers and Donald
Trump's defense lawyers, Bernie Sanders sent

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:34.001
up a really simple question, and it completely
set off attorney Michael Van Der Veen, one

00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:36.050
of Donald Trump's attorneys.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.860
And the question was simply, did Trump lie
about winning the election?

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.750
Is it true what the House managers said, which
is that for months Donald Trump wrongly claimed

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.330
to have won the election?

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:54.210
Bernie asked that Vanderveer went nuts, asking
who asked that and a titillating exchange

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.210
took place.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.210
So we'll take a look.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.410
The question is read by the clerk, but it's
Bernie's question that Bernie sent up.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.470
And when Van der Veen asks, who asks that
you will hear Bernie in the background say,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.270
I did, and then all hell breaks loose.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:14.130
And counsel for the former president recognized
for two and a half minutes right again and

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:22.439
not happened, of course, the clerk will read
the question again.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:28.280
The House prosecutors have stated over and
over again that President Trump was perpetrating

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:34.359
a big lie when he repeatedly claimed that
the election was stolen from him and that

00:01:34.359 --> 00:01:38.329
he actually won the election by a landslide.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:44.090
Are the prosecutors right when they claim
that Trump was telling a big lie, or in your

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.079
judgment, did Trump actually win the election?

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:51.889
The counsel for the former president have
two and a half minutes.

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:58.920
My judgment was that my judgment is irrelevant
in this proceeding.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.459
It absolutely is.

00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:06.069
What's supposed to happen here is the article
of impeachment is yesterday.

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:12.840
The Senate will be in order challenge.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:20.180
The senators under the rules, cannot challenge
the content of the response.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.780
The council will continue.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:29.099
Have the question read again, please.

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:34.819
The House prosecutors have stated over and
over again that President Trump was perpetrating

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:40.540
a big lie when he repeatedly claimed that
the election was stolen from him and that

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.890
he actually won the election by a landslide.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:49.360
Are the prosecutors right when they claim
that Trump was telling a big lie?

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.989
Or in your judgment, did Trump actually win
the election?

00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:59.689
In my judgment, it's irrelevant to the question
before this body.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:08.010
What's relevant in this impeachment article
is, were Mr. Trump's words insightful to the

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.599
point of violence and riot?

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:13.219
That's the charge.

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:14.760
That's the question.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.220
And the answer is no.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:23.360
So Michael van der Veen really not liking
Bernie's question, but it actually got Van

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.719
Der Veen to say something fascinating.

00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:32.859
You heard Van Der Veen there say the only
issue here is whether what Trump said was

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:35.330
an incitement to the riots.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.299
Many of the Republicans who voted not guilty,
including Mitch McConnell, as we just covered,

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:42.670
said Trump did incite the riots.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.030
But that's not the question.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.770
So I this is not trivial.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.970
Understand how disjointed and pathetic this
all is.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:56.459
Trump's own lawyer, when asked by Bernie,
was Trump lying when he said he won the election,

00:03:56.459 --> 00:04:04.319
says the only only only thing being adjudicated
here is did Donald Trump incite the riots?

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:08.530
Mitch McConnell comes in and says not guilty.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.010
Yes, Trump incited the riots.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.010
Yes.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.219
What Trump did is morally indefensible.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:18.269
Yes, what Trump did was foreseeable in leading
to what it led to.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:19.269
But that's not the issue.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:23.630
The issue is simply whether we can convict
the president that's already out of office.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:30.199
Even Trump's own lawyer there says the only
question is whether Trump incited the riot.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.069
And if yes, then presumably you vote guilty,
but Mitch votes not guilty and then says Trump

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:35.330
didn't cite the riot.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.690
But it's a procedural explanation for why
I voted not guilty.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:44.490
They aren't even in agreement about what this
trial was about.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:50.650
Now, to deal with the other part, there are
people who wrote to me and said it was funny

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:56.590
to see Vandar being triggered by Bernie's
question, but that factual question isn't

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.710
actually relevant to these proceedings.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:05.990
Now, strictly speaking, it is true that the
lawyer's opinion about a matter like that

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.980
isn't relevant to whether Trump is or isn't
guilty.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.620
But remember that this isn't a court of law.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.530
This is an impeachment court in the U.S. Senate.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.569
And adhering to the truth is relevant.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:24.760
Much of the case that Vanderveer and Trump's
other lawyers made didn't adhere to the truth.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.660
So merely and remember, it's not a criminal
proceeding.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:34.350
Senators can ask anything as a check on the
validity and comprehension of these lawyers.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.720
It was interesting to hear Bernie ask such
a basic factual question.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:43.930
It's also relevant that in the entire context
in which Trump's actions are being interpreted

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:50.819
in terms of his inciting the riot, it would
be different if, in reality, Trump had won

00:05:50.819 --> 00:05:51.830
Trump may.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:56.509
I'm not saying that the moral consideration
about the riot would necessarily be different.

00:05:56.509 --> 00:06:01.910
That would be for each senator to think about
and judge and for us if the election really

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.879
had been stolen from Trump.

00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:08.850
I think there are tens of millions of people,
including many on the left, who would see

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.950
the entire thing differently.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.080
So an interesting moment for sure.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.910
And Vanderveer just I mean, like instantly
hair trigger set off by Bernie's question.

